UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 30, Lesson 1: Safety at home and school', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Safety at home and school includes simple habits: keep walkways clear, walk inside, use equipment properly, keep medicines and cleaning products out of reach, ask an adult before using hot or sharp things, know the fire exit and the emergency number (000) and follow school rules in the playground and classroom. Knowing what to do in an emergency, such as staying low in smoke and meeting at a safe place, helps people stay calm.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWrite three hazards you might see in a kitchen and what you should do about each.\n\nFEEDBACK\nHazards: a hot pan handle sticking out, a wet floor and a sharp knife. Actions: tell an adult to turn the handle in, wipe the floor or put up a wet-floor sign, and ask an adult to put the knife away. Spotting hazards early prevents injuries.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking small hazards do not matter. Many injuries happen from small hazards like wet floors or bags in walkways.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWith an adult, walk through your home or classroom and list five hazards, one action for each and who is responsible. Draw an escape plan with a meeting place and write a 000 call script: which service, the address and what happened.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Safety at home and school includes simple habits: keep walkways clear, walk inside, use equipment properly, keep medicines and cleaning products out of reach, ask an adult before using hot or sharp things, know the fire exit and the emergency number (000) and follow school rules in the playground and classroom. Knowing what to do in an emergency, such as staying low in smoke and meeting at a safe place, helps people stay calm.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn and discuss","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-IHW-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: With an adult, walk through your home or classroom and list five hazards, one action for each and who is responsible. Draw an escape plan with a meeting place and write a 000 call script: which service, the address and w Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Hazards: a hot pan handle sticking out, a wet floor and a sharp knife. Actions: tell an adult to turn the handle in, wipe the floor or put up a wet-floor sign, and ask an adult to put the knife… Reasoning: I used a hazard by attending to an emergency plan rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking small hazards do not matter. Many injuries happen from small hazards like wet floors or bags in walkways. Correction: Hazards: a hot pan handle sticking out, a wet floor and a sharp knife. Actions: tell an adult to turn the handle in, wipe the floor or put up a wet-floor… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a hazard.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking small hazards do not matter. Many injuries happen from small hazards like wet floors or bags in walkways. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a hazard on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a hazard, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Hazards: a hot pan handle sticking out, a wet floor and a sharp knife. Actions: tell an adult to turn the handle in, wipe the floor or put up a wet-floor… This matches the lesson skill for a hazard.","options":["Copy the example and call that independent work.","Hazards: a hot pan handle sticking out, a wet floor and a sharp knife. Actions: tell an adult to turn the handle in, wipe the floor or put up a wet-floor…","Thinking small hazards do not matter. Many injuries happen from small hazards like wet floors or bags in walkways.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting."],"question":"Write three hazards you might see in a kitchen and what you should do about each. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Thinking small hazards do not matter. Many injuries happen from small hazards like wet floors or bags in walkways. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Hazards: a hot pan handle sticking out, a wet floor and a sharp knife. Actions: tell an adult to turn the handle in, wipe the floor or put up a wet-floor…","Careful use of an emergency plan with clear evidence.","Thinking small hazards do not matter. Many injuries happen from small hazards like wet floors or bags in walkways.","Safety at home and school includes simple habits: keep walkways clear, walk inside, use equipment properly, keep medicines and cleaning products out of reach,…"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising a hazard?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named an emergency plan, not by copying the model.","options":["Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the an emergency plan used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"A learner is practising a hazard. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Hazards: a hot pan handle sticking out, a wet floor and a sharp knife. Actions: tell an adult to turn the handle in, wipe the floor or put up a wet-floor… uses safe, healthy, respectful choices appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Thinking small hazards do not matter. Many injuries happen from small hazards like wet floors or bags in walkways.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Hazards: a hot pan handle sticking out, a wet floor and a sharp knife. Actions: tell an adult to turn the handle in, wipe the floor or put up a wet-floor…"],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about a hazard?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Thinking small hazards do not matter. Many injuries happen from small hazards like wet floors or bags in walkways.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about a hazard.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about an emergency plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Example: a home safety plan. Spot hazards: a bag in the hallway (move it), a hot kettle (ask an adult), medicines on a low shelf (tell an…","Thinking small hazards do not matter. Many injuries happen from small hazards like wet floors or bags in walkways."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Safety at home and school includes simple habits: keep walkways clear, walk inside, use equipment properly,… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["With an adult, walk through your home or classroom and list five hazards, one action for each and who is responsible. Draw an escape plan with a…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Thinking small hazards do not matter. Many injuries happen from small hazards like wet floors or bags in walkways."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of a hazard?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a hazard on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Hazards: a hot pan handle sticking out, a wet floor and a sharp knife. Actions: tell an adult to turn the handle in, wipe the floor or put up a wet-floor sign, and ask an adult to put the knife… Reasoning: I used a hazard by attending to an emergency plan rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with a hazard, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking small hazards do not matter. Many injuries happen from small hazards like wet floors or bags in walkways. Correction: Hazards: a hot pan handle sticking out, a wet floor and a sharp knife. Actions: tell an adult to turn the handle in, wipe the floor or put up a wet-floor… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a hazard.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w30-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a hazard.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Healthy, Safe and Active Lifestyles","success_criteria":["I can use a hazard with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify an emergency plan and explain why it matters.","I can practise calling 000 independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a meeting place needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Safety at home and school includes simple habits: keep walkways clear, walk inside, use equipment properly, keep medicines and cleaning products out of reach, ask an adult before using hot or sharp things, know the fire exit and the emergency number (000) and follow school…","options":["Safety at home and school includes simple habits: keep walkways clear, walk inside, use equipment properly, keep medicines and cleaning products out of reach, ask an adult before using hot or sharp things, know the fire exit and the emergency number (000) and follow school rules in the playground and classroom. Knowing what to do in an emergency, such as staying low in smoke and meeting at a safe place, helps people stay calm.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a hazard?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a home safety plan. Spot hazards: a bag in the hallway (move it), a hot kettle (ask an adult), medicines on a low shelf (tell an adult to move them). Emergency plan: test the smoke alarm, meet at the letterbox, call 000 and say ''fire'' and the address. At school: walk in the corridor and carry scissors with the points down.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite three hazards you might see in a kitchen and what you should do about each.\n\nFEEDBACK\nHazards: a hot pan handle sticking out, a wet floor and a sharp knife. Actions: tell an adult to turn the handle in, wipe the floor or put up a wet-floor sign, and ask an adult to put the knife away. Spotting hazards early prevents injuries.","explain":"Safety at home and school includes simple habits: keep walkways clear, walk inside, use equipment properly, keep medicines and cleaning products out of reach, ask an adult before using hot or sharp things, know the fire exit and the emergency number (000) and follow school rules in the playground and classroom. Knowing what to do in an emergency, such as staying low in smoke and meeting at a safe place, helps people stay calm.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking small hazards do not matter. Many injuries happen from small hazards like wet floors or bags in walkways. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Home hazards and actions.","caption":"Spot hazards early.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Hazards</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">bag, hot pan, wet floor</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Action</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">move it, tell an adult</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: a home safety plan. Spot hazards: a bag in the hallway (move it), a hot kettle (ask an adult), medicines on a l Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: a home safety plan. Spot hazards: a bag in the hallway (move it), a hot kettle (ask an adult), medicines on a l Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a hazard?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a home safety plan. Spot hazards: a bag in the hallway (move it), a hot kettle (ask an adult), medicines on a low shelf (tell an adult to move them). Emergency plan: test the smoke alarm, meet at the letterbox, call 000 and say ''fire'' and the address. At school: walk in the corridor and carry scissors with the points down. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"An emergency plan.","caption":"Know the plan.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Plan</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">meet at the letterbox</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Call</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">000, fire, address</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a hazard?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking small hazards do not matter. Many injuries happen from small hazards like wet floors or bags in walkways. Here is a more helpful example: Example: a home safety plan. Spot hazards: a bag in the hallway (move it), a hot kettle (ask an adult), medicines on a low shelf (tell an adult to move them). Emergency plan: test the smoke alarm, meet at the letterbox, call 000 and say ''fire'' and the address. At school: walk in the corridor and carry scissors with the points down.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nHazards: a hot pan handle sticking out, a wet floor and a sharp knife. Actions: tell an adult to turn the handle in, wipe the floor or put up a wet-floor sign, and ask an adult to put the knife away. Spotting hazards early prevents injuries.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking small hazards do not matter. Many injuries happen from small hazards like wet floors or bags in walkways. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a hazard?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSafety at home and school includes simple habits: keep walkways clear, walk inside, use equipment properly, keep medicines and cleaning products out of reach, ask an adult before using hot or sharp things, know the fire exit and the emergency number (000) and follow school rules in the playground and classroom. Knowing what to do in an emergency, such as staying low in smoke and meeting at a safe place, helps people stay calm. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 30, Lesson 1: Safety at home and school","unit_number":6,"unit_title":"Staying safe","week":30,"week_in_unit":5,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: a home safety plan. Spot hazards: a bag in the hallway (move it), a hot kettle (ask an adult), medicines on a low shelf (tell an adult to move them). Emergency plan: test the smoke alarm, meet at the letterbox, call 000 and say ''fire'' and the address. At school: walk in the corridor and carry scissors with the points down.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Home hazards and actions.","caption":"Spot hazards early.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Hazards</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">bag, hot pan, wet floor</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Action</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">move it, tell an adult</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"An emergency plan.","caption":"Know the plan.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\